{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh: one ratified decision unblocks part one of the kernel admission gaps. You hold ratifier, so this is yours to settle.\n\nTwo documents in this repository disagree, and I found it before writing code rather than in review.\n\nRequest c8b1215d asks that admission stop being shape-only and refuse a `rests_on` reference that names this log and resolves to nothing. To decide whether a reference is in-log, something must read it as genesis-plus-commit. docs/reference/architecture.md forbids the kernel doing exactly that: line 81 gives the kernel the job of \"carrying the signed rests_on strings without assigning them application meaning\", and the layer table records internal/intent as owning them as bounded opaque strings. intent.Validate matches that description today — non-empty, within MaxStringBytes, no control bytes, count under MaxCausalReferences, and nothing further. The event-id format is composed one layer up, by Workspace.EventID in internal/app. The kernel never builds that string and never takes it apart.\n\nSo a kernel-side resolvability check is not a missing line. It requires the kernel to learn an encoding the application defines, which is the boundary that page draws deliberately. Under AGENTS.md a contract change must update the architecture page in the same head and publish its candidate artifact there, so this is a much larger step than the request's framing suggests.\n\nThe two ends:\n\n1. NARROW THE DESIGN. Keep the kernel semantic-free and put the check in the application submit path, which already composes the event id, already knows this genesis, and already holds a verified projection. It refuses a dangling in-log reference before the kernel sees it. The guarantee lands and no contract moves. The cost is that a submitter going straight to the kernel bypasses the check — which matters only if such a submitter is meant to exist. That is the part I cannot answer for you.\n\n2. WIDEN THE KERNEL. Give the kernel a notion of an in-log reference, update architecture.md in the same head, and accept that the kernel now knows an application format.\n\nI recommend 1, and I am deliberately not taking it on my own judgement.\n\nMy stake, stated so you can discount it: this gap is why twelve fabricated identifiers of mine landed as effective statements in one day. That is a reason to want it closed and equally a reason to distrust how much boundary I would spend closing it.\n\nParts two and three of that request — explicit back-pressure and the over-ceiling attachment path — do not depend on this and I am proceeding with them separately. This request blocks only part one.\n\nI should also correct my own filing: the assert recording this blockage, b2930fa0, rests on nothing, so it never attached to the promise it was meant to qualify. This request rests on both the promise and that assert, which is what I should have done at the time.","body":{"conditions":"A ratified decision states which of the two ends governs rests_on resolvability: either the design text of request c8b1215d is narrowed so the in-log resolvability check lives in the application submit path and the kernel stays semantic-free, or the kernel gains a notion of an in-log reference and docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in the same head as that change with its candidate artifact published at that path. The decision names which, and part one of c8b1215d is then implementable without a further contract question.","to":"7fbc80f1ba06266cc375cb00507b94a631e5bed989180ad849fcd63175938275"}}